{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgdwEYUEPncjnveEYsQgB", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_01RyvUuY9mPu4zs8KG4urMF9", "name": "Write", "input": {"file_path": "<work>\\spec.json", "content": "{\n  \"claim\": \"For the pure spherical p-spin model xi(x)=x^p at p=4, the landscape threshold energies E_infinity (where the typical Hessian shift reaches the edge of the semicircle), E_2 and E_1 (zeros of the annealed index-k complexity functions), and E_0 (the ground-state energy constant) follow from the paper's closed-form pure-case complexity functions by root finding.\",\n  \"quantities\": [\n    {\n      \"key\": \"E_infinity_p4\",\n      \"description\": \"E_infinity(xi) = 2*sqrt(xi''(1))/xi'(1) for xi(x)=x^4, i.e. 2*sqrt((p-1)/p) at p=4. Report the full-precision value.\",\n      \"units\": \"dimensionless (energy per spin)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"key\": \"E_2_p4\",\n      \"description\": \"For p=4, let J(t) = (1/2) * integral from t to -2 of sqrt(x^2-4) dx for t <= -2 (J=0 for t>-2), and I_1(u) = J(u*sqrt(p/(p-1))). For u <= -E_infinity, Theta_k(u) = (1/2)log(p-1) - (p-2)/(4(p-1)) u^2 - (k+1) I_1(u). E_2 = sup{a>0 : Theta_2(-a) >= 0}, i.e. the root a > E_infinity of Theta_2(-a)=0 (k=2, so the coefficient of I_1 is 3). Report the full-precision value.\",\n      \"units\": \"dimensionless (energy per spin)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"key\": \"E_1_p4\",\n      \"description\": \"Same setup as E_2_p4 with k=1 (coefficient of I_1 equal to 2): E_1 = sup{a>0 : Theta_1(-a) >= 0} for p=4, the root a > E_infinity of (1/2)log 3 - (1/6) a^2 - 2 J(-a*sqrt(4/3)) = 0. Report the full-precision value.\",\n      \"units\": \"dimensionless (energy per spin)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"key\": \"E_0_p4\",\n      \"description\": \"Ground-state constant E_0 for the pure p=4 spherical spin glass, with (1/N) min H_N -> -E_0. For the pure model (Auffinger-Ben Arous-Cerny), this equals the root a > 